Use querySelector with a CSS selector for the first match, or getElementById when the element has an id.

code:
const button = document.querySelector('#submit');
const title = document.getElementById('page-title');

real example:
Playwright locators mirror this with page.locator('#submit'), selecting the same elements the page DOM exposes.

querySelectorAll returns a static NodeList of all matches, which you can convert to an array with Array.from or spread.

code:
const rows = document.querySelectorAll('.table-row');
const list = [...rows];
console.log(list.length);

real example:
Counting rows after a table re-renders uses querySelectorAll length, the same dataset Playwright count() asserts.

Create with createElement, fill its properties, then attach with appendChild or insertBefore.

code:
const li = document.createElement('li');
li.textContent = 'New item';
document.querySelector('#list').appendChild(li);

real example:
Injected overlay helpers built for test-only UI reuse this exact create and append pattern.

Use addEventListener with the event type and a callback, allowing multiple listeners on the same element.

code:
const btn = document.querySelector('#pay');
btn.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('clicked'));

real example:
Playwright exposes page.on for page events, which is a similar listener pattern for requests and dialogs.

The event object carries details about the event, such as target, currentTarget, and properties like key or which button was pressed.

code:
btn.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  console.log(event.target.id);
});

real example:
Form submit handlers read event.target to reach the input values without extra querying.

Event delegation attaches one listener to a parent and catches events from children via bubbling, which works for dynamically added elements and needs fewer listeners.

code:
document.querySelector('#list').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  if (e.target.closest('li')) console.log('row clicked');
});

real example:
Testing row clicks in tables that re-render pagination is stable when a single delegated listener handles all rows.

Access the input element and read its value property, which gives the current user-entered text for text, textarea, and select fields.

code:
const email = document.querySelector('#email').value;
console.log(email);

real example:
Playwright fill then assertions read the same value property when verifying what the user typed.

Assign the input's value property, and for controlled frameworks also dispatch an input event so the app sees the change.

code:
const input = document.querySelector('#name');
input.value = 'Ana';
input.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));

real example:
Setting a value directly bypasses user event paths, which is why React apps need the dispatched event for state to update.

window.location exposes href, pathname, host, search, and hash for the current page, enabling URL-based assertions.

code:
console.log(window.location.pathname);
console.log(window.location.search);

real example:
Asserting a redirect landed on /checkout?step=1 reads location.pathname and search after an expected navigation.

Assign to window.location.href, call window.location.reload(), or use location.assign for a push of a new URL.

code:
window.location.href = '/orders';
window.location.reload();

real example:
A hard refresh helper before a stability check reloads the page to reset transient UI state.

Listen for the DOMContentLoaded event on document, or window.load when all resources including images finish loading.

code:
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  console.log('tree ready');
});

real example:
Analytics and feature flags initialized on DOMContentLoaded explain why automated clicks sometimes race early-start listeners.

preventDefault cancels the default browser action like a form submit or link navigation. stopPropagation stops the event from bubbling to ancestors.

code:
form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();  // stop the page reload
  doValidation();
});

real example:
Single-page apps intercept form submits this way; accidental native submit causes page reloads that break Playwright waits.

Attach load and error event listeners during script injection, then observe window state changes after the resource loads.

code:
async function loadScript(src) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const s = document.createElement('script');
    s.src = src;
    s.onload = resolve;
    s.onerror = () => reject(new Error('cdn failed'));
    document.head.appendChild(s);
  });
}

real example:
Waiting for an analytics SDK script before asserting its API avoids null-pointer flaky failures in dependency checks.
